    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To complete the Weibull formula, you can use the following equation:\n\nMean Lead Time = (Shape Parameter x Scale Parameter)^(1/Shape Parameter)\n\nAnd the standard deviation can be calculated using:\n\nStandard Deviation of Lead Time = Scale Parameter x Gamma(Shape Parameter, 1/Shape Parameter)\n\nWhere Gamma is the Gamma function.\n\nNow, regarding your concern about capturing the \"weirdness\" in the pattern, you're right to question whether the chosen distribution is accurately accounting for the massive, random spikes in lead time. This is where the concept of \"model adequacy\" comes in.\n\nOne way to assess model adequacy is to use a goodness-of-fit test, such as the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test or the Anderson-Darling test. These tests compare the empirical distribution of the data to the theoretical distribution you've chosen, and provide a measure of how well the model fits the data.\n\nFor example, you can use the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test to calculate a p-value, which indicates the probability of observing the empirical distribution by chance. If the p-value is low (e.g., < 0.01), it suggests that the model is not adequately capturing the data.",
